alsa mixer: set and read the volume in the event loop's thread

The AlsaMixerMonitor calls snd_mixer_handle_events() in the event loop's thread,
while SetVolume() and GetVolume() use the same snd_mixer_t from the thread of the
client command. alsa-lib's mixer interface is not thread-safe: selem_read() zeroes
an element's volumes before it reads them again.

Setting the volume of a stereo control is two writes, one per channel. The first
write makes the kernel send a change event, the monitor handles it at once and
re-reads the element, and the second write, running at the same moment in the other
thread, can pick up a zero or the previous volume for the first channel.

Seen in QEMU with an emulated USB sound card, where control transfers are slow: of
150 volume changes, 3 ended with one or both channels at 0 instead of the volume
asked for (measured with the previous patch applied).

Run both functions in the event loop's thread with BlockingCall(), as the monitor's
destructor already does.

Upstream: not submitted yet. Unpatched MPD sets the volume with the same kind of
per-channel alsa-lib call (snd_mixer_selem_set_playback_dB_all), so it should have the
same race; that was not measured.

--- a/src/mixer/plugins/AlsaMixerPlugin.cxx
+++ b/src/mixer/plugins/AlsaMixerPlugin.cxx
@@ -309,19 +309,22 @@
 int
 AlsaMixer::GetVolume()
 {
-	int err;
-
 	assert(handle != nullptr);
 
-	err = snd_mixer_handle_events(handle);
-	if (err < 0)
-		throw Alsa::MakeError(err, "snd_mixer_handle_events() failed");
+	int volume = -1;
 
-	int volume = GetPercentVolume();
-	if (resulting_volume >= 0 && volume == resulting_volume)
-		/* we're still on the value passed to SetVolume() */
-		volume = desired_volume;
+	/* in the thread of the AlsaMixerMonitor; see SetVolume() */
+	BlockingCall(event_loop, [this, &volume](){
+		int err = snd_mixer_handle_events(handle);
+		if (err < 0)
+			throw Alsa::MakeError(err, "snd_mixer_handle_events() failed");
 
+		volume = GetPercentVolume();
+		if (resulting_volume >= 0 && volume == resulting_volume)
+			/* we're still on the value passed to SetVolume() */
+			volume = desired_volume;
+	});
+
 	return volume;
 }
 
@@ -330,17 +333,26 @@
 {
 	assert(handle != nullptr);
 
-	/* the same position on the mixer's own scale: 40% is 40% of its range */
-	long min = 0, max = 0;
-	int err = snd_mixer_selem_get_playback_volume_range(elem, &min, &max);
-	if (err >= 0)
-		err = snd_mixer_selem_set_playback_volume_all(elem,
-							      min + lround(0.01 * volume * double(max - min)));
-	if (err < 0)
-		throw Alsa::MakeError(err, "failed to set ALSA volume");
+	/* The AlsaMixerMonitor calls snd_mixer_handle_events() on this
+	   mixer handle in the event loop's thread, and alsa-lib's mixer
+	   is not safe to use from two threads: while it re-reads an
+	   element it has the element's volumes zeroed. Setting a
+	   stereo volume is two writes, and the first one triggers such
+	   a re-read; the second write could then store a zero, or the
+	   old volume, for the other channel. So do it in that thread. */
+	BlockingCall(event_loop, [this, volume](){
+		/* the same position on the mixer's own scale: 40% is 40% of its range */
+		long min = 0, max = 0;
+		int err = snd_mixer_selem_get_playback_volume_range(elem, &min, &max);
+		if (err >= 0)
+			err = snd_mixer_selem_set_playback_volume_all(elem,
+								      min + lround(0.01 * volume * double(max - min)));
+		if (err < 0)
+			throw Alsa::MakeError(err, "failed to set ALSA volume");
 
-	desired_volume = volume;
-	resulting_volume = GetPercentVolume();
+		desired_volume = volume;
+		resulting_volume = GetPercentVolume();
+	});
 }
 
